n opening with emerson's  letter which whitman never got  permission to use it also used his  quotes on the spine and basically  advertised the crap out of its  connection with the more famous man  remarkably it also contained an  additional 20 new poems including the  perpetual favorite later renamed  crossing brooklyn ferry but while other  writers like henry david thoreau would  be bowled over by this edition the  public still wasn't buying and that was  a serious problem because whitman was by  now broke when thoreau went to visit  whitman he found the poet living in the  family home in brooklyn now marked by  squalor and misery like their father  before them two of whitman's brothers  were alcoholics while his sister was  suffering poor mental health so far was  the family that whitman himself was  sharing a bed with a brother clearly no  amount of friendly endorsements from  famous poets could make up for having an  actual income hence whitman's brief  return to journalism in 1857 brief  because within two years have been fired  again this time over an article  supporting women's sexual freedom broke  once more he tried to find a serious  publisher for his third edition of  leaves remarkably he actually succeeded  landing in december 1860 the third  edition is where whitman's poems hit  their scandalous peak with a section  called children of adam that openly  celebrated heterosexual love and another  calamus that seemed to allude to  romantic love between men it was also  the addition that seemed bound for  success the one that would finally make  a splash with the public only things  didn't quite work out rather than  starting 1861 as a newly successful poet  women was destined to start 1861 just  like everybody else in america as  someone whose life was about to get  flipped upside down  by the arrival of war  for the first two years of the civil war  what women's life is something of a  blank an empty room he seems to have  wandered into and disappeared we know  his publisher went bust early on taking  his hopes for leaves his third edition  down with it but it's not until december  1862 that the record becomes clearer  that's because 16th of december 1862 is  the day whitman opened the new york  herald and felt his life implode the day  before the battle of fredericksburg had  ended with one of the union's highest  casualty counts of the entire civil war  12 653 missing dead or wounded and now  hear walt whitman was staring at the  casualty list the list of names carrying  that of his brother george it's hard to  imagine what whitman was thinking as he  hurried to the dc area as he went from  hospital to hospital 40 in all each  packed with the wounded and dying it's  easy to imagine the relief when he  finally found george alive and well and  only lightly wounded but the experience  of getting so close to the war of seeing  its victims seems to have changed  something in women after accompanying a  small group of wounded soldiers back  from fredericksburg to the capitol  whitman made a profound decision he  decided to stay in early 1863 the 43  year old began the volunteering work  that would take up the next two years of  his life working in the military  hospitals providing conversation and  companionship to the wounded not being a  trained nurse his role was limited but  still on a good day women felt like he  was giving the injured men something to  cling to perhaps a reason to hold on  through the pain just a bit longer and  he really committed to it supporting  himself with part-time work in the  paymaster's office whitman used his  meager salary to buy small gifts for the  soldiers bringing them something nearly  every day today it's estimated that he  made around 600 trips offering tens of  thousands of men union and confederate  alike small morsels of comfort in return  his muse rewarded him with his greatest  work in years drum traps is a  mesmerizing poetic account of the civil  war from the early joy of fighting for a  just cause to the inescapable melancholy  of what combat really means the hurt the  blood the uncertain recovery aroused and  angry i'd thought to beat the alarm to  urge relentless war goes one verse but  soon my fingers failed me my face  drooped and i resigned myself to sit by  the wounded and sue them or silently  watch the dead it was exactly the return  to form that whitman needed capturing  what the civil war had been to so many  people who lived it yet in the end drum  taps would be overshadowed by a sequel  one written in light of horrific events  on april 15 1865 whitman was proofing  drum tabs when a man stepped into the  president's box at ford's theater and  shot abraham lincoln through the head  during his time volunteering in dc  hospitals women had become acquainted  with lincoln who'd read and enjoyed  leaves of grass and the two occasionally  dipped hats in the street so when news  reached the poet of the president's  death women did the only thing he could  do  he wrote about it wrote to give the dead  man an allergy wrote to express  everything the grieving nation was  feeling collected in the sequel to drum  taps later that same year the four poems  whitman wrote on lincoln's death remain  some of his best and best known work  from the painful epic of when lilac's  last in the door yard bloomed to the  short mournful o captain my captain the  poems for the first time brought whitman  to wide attention sadly though they did  so with the perverse logic of a cursed  monkey's poor the president women had  looked up to one had called the great  martyr chief was gone just as hundreds  of thousands of young men were victims  of the war while the coming years would  bring whitman fame they'd also bring a  hint of something alien to this most  optimistic of men a sense of creeping  disappointment in the world around him  [Music]  one of the best things to happen to  whitman in this period of national  mourning was getting on the wrong side  of james harlan the secretary of the  interior harlan was in charge of  downsizing the government but he was  also something of a massive crude a  prude who'd read leaves of grass  presumably terrified at the stirring  feeling he felt in his pants and was now  determined that whitman was to be  expelled from washington the firing of  whitman from his government post was the  exact sort of boost that his career  needed outraged at what they saw as  government censorship but byron sprang  to his defense within 24 hours had  gotten a new job at the attorney  general's office on top of that william  douglas o'connor then published a  pamphlet denouncing harlan as a censor  and portraying whitman as a modern  christ who gave his life to protect the  first amendment as a result a woman  became almost a household name when the  fourth edition of leaves dropped in 1867  it recorded decent sales for the first  time however this version of leaves  didn't just feature the newest poems  including drum taps it also chopped out  a handful from the third edition a  handful of poems that were among the  most scandalous poems that seem to  explicitly deal with romantic sexual  love between men now before we start  down this path we need to be clear that  we have no direct proof that whitman was  what we'd now call gay or queer terms  that didn't exist in his time on the  other hand we have so much  circumstantial and indirect evidence  that it quickly piles up into a fabulous  mountain that's nearly impossible to  deny first there are the live oak poems  that featured in the third edition of  leaves only to be later removed and go  without republication for a hundred  years part of the calamus set written  while whitman was living with a  strapping young irish lad named fred  vaughn the live oak poems are generally  thought to be about a relationship  between the two poem five for example is  a long declaration that whitman will  stop writing about america because he  has no time now that i have found him  who loves me as i him in  perfect love and also includes the lines  i am indifferent to my own songs i am to  go with him i love and he is to go with  me it is to be enough for each of us  that we are together we never separate  again meanwhile pern 8 is thought to be  written after they broke up with a  section that reads hours discouraged  distracted for he the one i cannot  content myself without soon i saw him  content himself without me remember this  is on top of the non-removed poems in  calamus that still refer to sexy dude on  dude love albeit in a slightly less  direct way there are other hints too  such as the letters whitman wrote about  some of the soldiers he tended to while  volunteering in dc describing their  splendid necks frames and clean  complexions and strong bodies but  perhaps the strongest argument for  whitman's same-sex attraction is peter  doyle another young irishman doyle had  fought for the confederacy surviving the  war to become a train car conductor he  met whitman while working and the two  more or less immediately began a  decade-long relationship that many  consider to have been romantic in nature  certainly whitman was fond of referring  to doyle as my darling or my darling boy  in his letters and the two spent long  periods in the 1860s and 1870s  cohabiting with doyle even acting as  whitman's carer when his health  collapsed today it's assumed that doyle  was whitman's lover probably even the  greatest love of his life but with  same-sex attraction being so taboo in  those days it's impossible to say for  sure they simply left no hard evidence  no diaries no signed confession that  reads dear future scholars oh we were  totally boning so all we can do is try  to read between the lines and see the  traces of a love story that no one in  the era would have dead right but even  if this period saw walt whitman the  lover consigned to invisibility the same  could only be said of whitman the poet  because the years he spent with doyle  would also coincide with whitman finally  becoming world famous  [Music]  as the 1860s ticked towards their end  more and more readers were beginning to  take notice of the bearded bare-chested  satire of american letters partially  this was due to the success of drum taps  and the lincoln elegies but it was also  due to a new generation of scholars  championing whitman's work 1967 saw  notes on what whitman as poet and person  reignite scholarly interest while 1868  saw the first british edition of his  poems published interestingly for a poet  as solidly american as whitman the brits  went nuts over his verse by the  mid-1870s after the publication of  leaves his fifth edition it'd be so  popular in the uk that british critics  were able to write long articles calling  americans uncultured swine for ignoring  this genius in their midst but while  whitman's star might at last have been  rising  things weren't all perfect 1873 saw the  suffer his first stroke leaving him  partially paralyzed and forced to  eventually quit his government job and  move in with his brother george come the  end of the decade he and peter doyle had  likewise drifted apart although they'd  remain in contact and are thought to  have restarted their relationship at one  point what was perhaps the greatest  romance of whitman's life was now over  yet it would be a third potential  catastrophe that wound up sealing  whitman's reputation all right so  remember possible james harlan getting  so worked up about whitman's immoral  poetry well apparently he wasn't the  only one to get his knickers in a twist  over the more risque parts of leaves in  1882 just as the sixth edition was about  to go on sale the boston district  attorney announced that he was banning  the book he even tried to ban the act of  sending it bar mail if you ever read  whitman's poems in school you might be  wondering what the fuss was about this  wasn't exactly naked lunch but this is  the past of course they do things  differently there so women was forced to  find a new publisher outside of boston  all the while the press talked up this  immoral book until everyone on earth had  heard of the controversy the result  inevitably was to make the sixth edition  a smash hit for the first time in his  life whitman made enough money to buy  himself a little house in camden new  jersey there he settled into the final  act of his life as a famous poet at last  able to rest on his laurels these were  the twilight years the years in which  people like the irish poet oscar wilde  made pilgrimages across the atlantic to  meet the great writer the years in which  he was at last able to bask in the glow  of recognition yet even now there was  still a dark cloud forever threatening  to obscure whitman's sunny optimism as  the 1880s creaked into the 1890s women  became disenchanted with america's  direction with the land that had  rhapsodized as a young man a place he  came to believe had taken too many wrong  turns after the civil war after it lost  its greatest captain not the whitman  would live to see the rest of the  journey after a long winter of illness  what whitman died on march 26 1892 at  the age of 72. by this point it was so  famous that papers like the new york  times kept a bedside vigil updating  their readers each day on the dying  man's prognosis when the time came for  the final update it was like the curtain  had just fallen over an entire era but  the moment he died women left behind one  of the most remarkable poetic legacies  of all time over six official editions  and three reissues leaves of grass had  come to encompass nearly 300 poems each  a testament to a wholly new way of  writing of seeing the world so perfect  were they that rather than fade he only  grew in fame after his death until in  the here and now his name stands at the  pinnacle of american poetry up there  with edgar allan poe robert frost or  emily dickinson and maybe perhaps above  them all it's pleasing in a way that a  man so defiantly outside the  establishment a political street fighter  who wrote in vernacular and likely took  male lovers would wind up becoming the  most established poets the one everyone  has to learn about in school and make no  mistake he deserves to be there what  women may not have quite invented free  verse but he arguably invented something  even greater a form of poetry of seeing  the world that was unique to his beloved  homeland  [Music]  you
 

